<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n18616" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">fornĭcātus</span></b> , a,
		um, adj.
		fornix, I., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>vaulted</i>, <i>arched.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">paries vel solidus vel fornicatus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Top. 4, 22</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ambitus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					12, 5, 11, § 22</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> As
			an <i>adj. prop.</i>: Via Fornicata, or <i>Arch Street</i>, <i>a street in Rome
				leading to the</i> Campus Martius, <i><span class="en">Liv. 22,
				36, 8</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
